    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d609">

                <pb facs="0672" n="568" sameAs="frus:frus1932v03/pg568"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">793.94/4704: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Minister in Switzerland (Wilson) to the Secretary of State</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Geneva</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1932-03-11T20:00:00+01:00">March 11, 1932—8
                        p.m.</date><lb/>[Received March 11—2:30 p.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>73. Assembly passed resolution<anchor n="90" xml:id="d609-f1"/> unchanged from
                    text reported in Gilbert’s 116, March 11,
                    2 p.m.<anchor n="91" xml:id="d609-f2"/></p>
                <p>Drummond handed me at once letter of which
                    text follows: <quote rend="blockquote">
                        <p>“I beg to enclose herewith for the information of your Government the
                            text of a resolution relative to the Sino-Japanese dispute which was
                            adopted this afternoon by the Assembly of the League of Nations.</p>
                        <p>In view of the collaboration which has on several occasions in the course
                            of recent events been effected between the Government of the United
                            States and the governments of other powers having special interests in
                            the Shanghai Settlement, perhaps you will allow me to draw your
                            particular attention to part II of the Assembly resolution in which the
                            Assembly addresses a request to the powers members of the League of
                            Nations which have such special interests.”</p></quote></p>
                <p>In giving me this letter he explained that it seemed more tactful not to ask for
                    an answer from the American Government but merely to draw attention to part II
                    of the resolution, but that of course if you cared to give an answer it would
                    complete the record of cooperation which has been maintained throughout this
                    negotiation.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                            >Wilson</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
